on and Tanigawa (2024) initiated the study of maximal matroids with prescribed sets of circuits, conjecturing that certain well-known abstract rigidity matroids are unique maximal matroids for specific simple circuit families. This paper disproves two of these conjectures by constructing counterexamples.\n\nA central concept is the $X$X-matroid, a matroid $M$M on ground set $E$E where every set in a given family $X \\subseteq 2^E$X⊆2 E is a circuit of $M$M. Matroids are ordered by inclusion of their independent sets. A maximal $X$X-matroid is one that is maximal in this poset. Jackson and Tanigawa (2024) introduced a function $\\mathrm{val}_X: 2^E \\to \\mathbb{Z}$val X​:2 E→Z as an upper bound on the rank function of any $X$X-matroid. For a proper $X$X-sequence $S = (X_1, \\dots, X_k)$S=(X 1​,…,X k​) (where $X_i \\in X$X i​∈X and $X_i \\not\\subseteq X_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup X_{i-1}$X i​⊆X 1​∪⋯∪X i−1​), $\\mathrm{val}(F, S) = |F \\cup X_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup X_k| - k$val(F,S)=∣F∪X 1​∪⋯∪X k​∣−k. Then, $\\mathrm{val}_X(F) = \\min\\{\\mathrm{val}(F, S) : S \\text{ is a proper } X\\text{-sequence}\\}$val X​(F)=min{val(F,S):S is a proper X-sequence}. A key result [JT24, Lemma 1.2] states that if $\\mathrm{val}_X$val X​ is submodular and an $X$X-matroid exists, then there is a unique maximal $X$X-matroid whose rank function coincides with $\\mathrm{val}_X$val X​. Disproving that a specific matroid is the unique maximal $X$X-matroid consequently implies that $\\mathrm{val}_X$val X​ is not its rank function.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBalanced counterexamples to Ryser’s conjecture at prime orders | Plectis Atlas (https://wcook04.github.io/plectis-atlas/papers/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026.html)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; For $r=2$r=2, inequality (1) is Kőnig’s matching theorem for bipartite graphs. ... Ryser’s Conjecture for Tripartite 3-Graphs. ... Tuza’s Ryser-conjecture claim for four-partite hypergraphs with matching number two. arXiv:2609.14281v1, 2026.\n\n# Balanced counterexamples to Ryser’s conjecture at prime orders\n\nOpenAI, 27 September 2026\n\nRead the PDFTeX source[Button: Copy OpenAI’s citation]\n\nOpenAI’s paper, from its mathematics release (openai/math, Apache License 2.0). Plectis Atlas, which is not affiliated with OpenAI, converted its TeX into this page; the text and the statement numbers can differ from the PDF, the version to cite. One figure is shown as a placeholder; see the PDF for it.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor every sufficiently large prime $q$q, we construct a finite intersecting $(q+1)$(q+1)-partite $(q+1)$(q+1)-uniform hypergraph with covering number $q+1$q+1 and exactly $q+1$q+1 nonisolated vertices in each part. This disproves Ryser’s covering conjecture, even for intersecting hypergraphs with equally sized parts.\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nA vertex cover of a finite hypergraph $H$H is a set of vertices meeting every edge. Its minimum possible size is the covering number $\\tau(H)$τ(H). The matching number $\\nu(H)$ν(H) is the maximum number of pairwise disjoint edges. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting paths, cycles, and blow‐ups in planar graphs - Cox - 2022 - Journal of Graph Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.22838)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; ### 5.2 Edge-blow-ups\n\nThe question of determining $ $\\beta (H,k)$$ is wide open for most graphs $ $H$$. ... Conjecture 5.5.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Color-Dressed Generalized Biadjoint Scalar Amplitudes: Local Planarity (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366497743_Color-Dressed_Generalized_Biadjoint_Scalar_Amplitudes_Local_Planarity)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: 5